<description>
Keep only the unique largest solid monochrome rectangle and erase the surrounding noise.

Input structure
- The grid has a black background with scattered non-background noise pixels.
- One non-background color contains a unique large solid axis-aligned rectangle,
  possibly with extra same-color protrusions outside the rectangle.
Rule
- Find the largest-area solid rectangle whose cells all share one non-background
  color and whose height and width are both at least two.
- Return a blank background grid with only that rectangle redrawn in its
  original position and color.
Key insight
- The retained color is identified by the large filled rectangular support,
  not by its color id or by total color frequency.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "non-background pixels on a black background, including one unique largest solid monochrome rectangle",
  "transform": "erase all pixels except that largest solid rectangle; preserve the rectangle's original color and position"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 16x16, black background
  colors: all 9 non-black colors (1-9) present
  target_rectangle:
    color: any one of the 9 colors
    dimensions: 2-4 rows x 3-7 cols, area 9-14
    solid monochrome, positioned anywhere with >=2 margin from edges [observed]
    must be the unique largest solid >=2x2 rectangle in the grid
  target_extra_pixels:
    count: 7-13 same-color isolated pixels scattered outside the rectangle
    no two adjacent (4-connected) to each other
  distractor_colors:
    count: 8 (all other non-black colors)
    pixels_per_color: 5-14
    no color may contain any solid >=2x2 rectangle (pixels are scattered)
    same-color adjacency occasionally occurs but no 2x2 block
  density: 28-40% of cells non-black
  output: same-size grid with only the target rectangle drawn; everything else black

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Create 16x16 black grid. Pick target color from 1-9.
    2. Place a solid rectangle (h in 2-4, w in 3-7, area 9-14) at a random position with >=2 cell margin from all edges. Fill output grid.
    3. Assign the remaining 8 colors to the other values from 1-9.
    4. For target color, scatter 7-13 isolated pixels outside the rectangle (no two 4-adjacent to each other or to the rectangle).
    5. For each distractor color, scatter 5-14 pixels randomly on remaining black cells, ensuring no color ever forms a solid 2x2 block. Allow occasional same-color adjacency.
    6. Verify the target rectangle is the unique largest solid >=2x2 rectangle across all colors (rejection check; virtually always passes since distractors have no 2x2 block).
```
